// @test: parse-fail
// @level: L0
// @tags: parser, proofs, tactic, error, T0983
// @expected-error: E029
// @description: A tactic proof body missing its closing brace must be refused, exactly as the structured form already is
//
// The sibling of `missing_qed.vr`, which pins the same mistake in the
// STRUCTURED proof form and passes:
//
//     proof { have step1: true by trivial          → E029, refused
//
// The tactic form was silent. `{ proof by smt` with no `}` parsed
// clean, and the next `theorem` closed the body — so the file below
// declared ONE theorem where its author wrote two, and the verifier
// reported on whichever set the recovery produced.
//
// Measured 2026-08-30 before the fix: a file with a missing brace and
// one with the brace present produced BYTE-IDENTICAL output, both
// reporting every theorem proved. In the proof corpus this had reached
// 24 missing braces in a single file (blast_tactic.vr) with all 38
// theorems still reported.
//
// Why it matters more here than in ordinary code: a spec is an
// ASSERTION ABOUT THE LANGUAGE. If its structure is decided by error
// recovery, then which theorems it declares is decided by a heuristic
// rather than by its author — and the file looks healthy either way.

theorem first(x: Int): x == x {
    proof by smt

theorem second(y: Int): y == y {
    proof by smt
}
